ROLE: Senior Unity C# Architect + Spine 2D Tech Artist.

PROJECT: Polyomino puzzle prototype.
- Whole screen is a snap grid (global grid).
- Central highlighted 4x4 is the "Board Area" only (ghost + obstacles live there).
- Piece can be placed anywhere on the screen grid (including partially outside the central board).

NON-NEGOTIABLE REQUIREMENTS
1) Global grid:
   - Candidate origin cell is always computed from pointer world position (WorldToCellRound).
   - Coordinates may be outside [0..3] and that is valid.
2) Central board behavior:
   - Ghost is visible ONLY if at least one shape cell intersects the central 4x4.
   - Ghost draws ONLY the intersecting cells inside [0..3]x[0..3].
   - Validity for those intersecting cells must reject blocked/occupied (treat blocked == occupied).
3) Drop behavior:
   - If placement is valid => snap to candidate cell (even outside board).
   - If invalid => bounce back to pre-drag position.
   - Remove any "push outside grid" parking logic.
4) Rotation:
   - Anchor-based rotation around pivot block (0,0 by default).
   - DO NOT normalize minX/minY after rotation.
   - Negative local cell coordinates are allowed.
   - Anchor block world position must remain unchanged after rotation.
5) Tweening:
   - Use PrimeTween for snap/bounce/rotate feel. Tweens must be cancellable.
6) Spine:
   - Using SkeletonAnimation (world-space).
   - Do NOT freeze SkeletonAnimation.timeScale to stop idle; stop only animation tracks (use SetEmptyAnimation/ClearTrack) so physics/constraints can continue.
   - Apply procedural bone overrides in SkeletonAnimation UpdateWorld callback.
   - Idle animation name is "animation".
7) Safety:
   - Do not rename MonoBehaviour classes or break serialized Inspector links.
   - Output patch-like edits per file and a manual test checklist after each stage.

WORKFLOW
A) Analyze first (no code).
B) Implement stage-by-stage minimal diffs, each stage compiles.
C) After each stage: list changed files + tests.
